---
// Minimal About page: who, why, principles, sources/licenses, privacy, corrections.
// Phase 4 expands it.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { correctionUrl, REPO, COUNTER_URL } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base title="About" description="Who built Station App Finder, why, where the data comes from, and how to correct it.">
  <div class="wrap narrow band stack gap-32">
    <section class="stack gap-12">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p>
        Station App Finder helps anyone find their local public media station and every way
        to watch and listen: the station's own apps for phone, tablet, and TV, and
        its website. It's an independent prototype.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="why" class="stack gap-12">
      <h2>Why this exists</h2>
      <p>
        Local public media stations make and share programs for their own communities: local
        news and public affairs, arts and culture, history, and learning for every age. They're
        free to watch over the air, and they're run by local nonprofits, universities, and public
        agencies that answer to the people they serve.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most stations now have their own apps, but they're hard to find. They're spread across
        many app stores and devices, often under names that don't match what people call their
        station, and many people don't know which station is theirs. This site puts every
        station's apps in one place, for every device, so it takes seconds to find your own.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack gap-12">
      <h2>Why the station's own app</h2>
      <ul class="stack gap-8">
        <li><strong>The best experience.</strong> A station's app brings together its livestream, its local shows, and its schedule, built for your phone, tablet, or TV.</li>
        <li><strong>Everything your station makes.</strong> National apps are great for national programs, but your station's app is where its local work lives.</li>
        <li><strong>A stronger local station.</strong> Watching in the station's own app keeps a direct connection between the station and its audience, and gives it a clearer picture of what its community values.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For any device a station's own app isn't on, we point you to the PBS app, so you can always watch.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack gap-12">
      <h2>How we list stations</h2>
      <ul class="stack gap-8">
        <li><strong>Neutral.</strong> Every station is listed and shown the same way. Results are ordered by distance or how well they match your search, nothing else.</li>
        <li><strong>Station first.</strong> We link a station's own apps. The PBS app appears only for devices the station's own app isn't on.</li>
        <li><strong>No endorsement.</strong> We aren't affiliated with or endorsed by PBS or NPR, and we use their names as text only.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="sources" class="stack gap-12">
      <h2>Where the information comes from</h2>
      <ul class="stack gap-8">
        <li>
          Stations, owners, websites, and transmitter locations: Wikipedia's{' '}
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_PBS_member_stations">List of PBS member stations</a>,{' '}
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_NPR_stations">List of NPR stations</a>,{' '}
          and each station's article, by Wikipedia contributors, used under{' '}
          <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. We extract and
          combine fields from those articles; the station data we publish is shared under the same license.
        </li>
        <li>App links: each station's own website. We don't scrape app stores.</li>
        <li>ZIP code locations: U.S. Census Bureau ZCTA Gazetteer (public domain).</li>
        <li>Every field records where it came from and when. The data and code are public at <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy" class="stack gap-12">
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        No cookies, no accounts, and no third-party trackers. Searches run in your browser.
        "Use my location" is optional, stays on your device, and is rounded before it's used.
      </p>
      {COUNTER_URL && (
        <>
          <p>
            We count events, never people: daily totals of page views by page type, searches
            (the kind of search, how many stations it found, and only the first three digits
            of a ZIP code or the state), and clicks out to a station's app or website (which
            station and which platform). Each comes with only a device type (phone, tablet,
            computer). Nothing is stored about individual visits: no search text, full ZIP
            codes, locations, cookies, IDs, or IP addresses. If your browser sends Do Not Track
            or Global Privacy Control, nothing is counted at all.
          </p>
        </>
      )}
    </section>

    <section class="stack gap-12">
      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>Something wrong or missing? <a href={correctionUrl()}>Suggest a correction</a>. Corrections are reviewed by hand and always win over automatically gathered data.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  ul { padding-left: 1.2em; margin: 0; }
</style>
